# SQL IS LF ON EVERY MACHINE, ALWAYS.
#
# 17 Oct 2026: replaying all 171 migrations onto a new Supabase project failed
# on 20260926090000. The cause was line endings, and it is worth writing down
# because nothing in the toolchain can see it:
#
#   * git stores every migration as LF, but `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows
#     clone checks 132 of them out as CRLF;
#   * several migrations edit a LIVE function body by asserted anchor
#     (`_dos_swap`) - they read `pg_get_functiondef()` and require a byte-exact
#     substring to appear exactly N times;
#   * a CRLF file stores its function body with \r\n, while the anchor in the
#     editing migration carries a plain \n (written as a literal \n escape).
#     The anchor then matches 0 times and the migration refuses - correctly,
#     but for a reason that has nothing to do with the rule it is protecting.
#
# Typecheck, lint and the build cannot see any of this. Pinning eol=lf is what
# makes the migration set replayable on any machine.
*.sql text eol=lf
